  "text": "Sure! Here's the `remove_adjacent_duplicates` function implemented correctly, along with the test cases as shown. The function iterates through the list and builds a new list, only adding an element if it is different from the previous one.\n\n```python\nimport unittest\n\ndef remove_adjacent_duplicates(lst):\n    if not lst:\n        return []\n    \n    result = [lst[0]]\n    for num in lst[1:]:\n        if num != result[-1]:\n            result.append(num)\n    return result\n\nclass TestRemoveAdjacentDuplicates(unittest.TestCase):\n    def test_basic_functionality(self):\n        self.assertEqual(remove_adjacent_duplicates([1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3, 4]), [1, 2, 3, 4])\n    \n    def test_repeated_elements(self):\n        self.assertEqual(remove_adjacent_duplicates([5, 5, 5, 6, 7, 7]), [5, 6, 7])\n    \n    def test_multiple_duplicates(self):\n        self.assertEqual(remove_adjacent_duplicates([1, 2, 3, 4, 4, 4, 4, 5, 5]), [1, 2, 3, 4, 5])\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    unittest.main()\n```\n\n### Explanation:\n- The function starts by checking if the input list is empty and returns an empty list in that case.\n- It initializes a `result` list with the first element of the input list.\n- It then loops through the rest of the elements in the input list and only appends an element to the result list if it is different from the last element in the result list.\n- This effectively removes consecutive duplicates by keeping only the first occurrence of each group of adjacent duplicates.\n\n### Example:\nInput: `[1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3, 4]`  \nOutput: `[1, 2, 3, 4]`\n\nLet me know if you'd like it to remove *all* duplicates regardless of adjacency, or if you want a version that handles more complex input (e.g., strings, etc.).",
